The term “safe” can be used in different contexts. It could mean:

an object or compartment that is secured to prevent loss of valuable contents, or to secure things or people that would otherwise be unsafe;

a strongbox or vault, especially one that is locked;

a safe place, as a place of refuge; or

an account that is protected by an insurance policy.

I’m not an expert on insults in general, but I can say that these things tend to be insulting for a Chinese person:

    – sarcastic, disrespectful language and tone, usually in response to any perceived disagreement or refusal to go along with their wishes;

    –  referring to a Hong Kong person as “boy”, “girl” or “girlfriend”;

    –  describing a Hong Kong person as “chinese”, which is a racist slur.
